[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C code uses a namespace macro:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)
// expands to: SPX_prf_addr
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the compiled C library exports the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m. The test runner dynamically loads the [38;5;10m.so[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m and looks for exactly that symbol name.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation correctly implemented the [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m logic as a private [38;5;10mfn prf_addr(...)[0m, but [1mnever exported it with the C-visible name [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m[22m. The shared library was missing the symbol entirely, causing a [38;5;10mdlsym[0m failure at runtime.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Added an [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m wrapper function right after the internal [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(
    out: *mut u8,
    ctx: *const SpxCtx,
    addr: *const [u32; 8],
) {
    let out_s = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, SPX_N) };
    let ctx_r = unsafe { &*ctx };
    let addr_r = unsafe { &*addr };
    prf_addr(out_s, ctx_r, addr_r);
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This gives the symbol the correct C ABI name ([38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m), matching what the runner expects, and delegates to the existing correct implementation. The underlying blake256 hash logic was already correct — it was purely a missing FFI export.[0m[0m
